<article class="env-example" id="0EL6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0EL6">Example <span data-tag="0EL6">52.28.6</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field and let $X$ be a proper variety over $k$. Let $Y \subset X$ be an effective Cartier divisor such that $\mathcal{O}_ X(Y)$ is ample and denote $\mathcal{I} \subset \mathcal{O}_ X$ the corresponding sheaf of ideals. Let $(\mathcal{E}_ n)$ an object of $\textit{Coh}(X, \mathcal{I})$ with $\mathcal{E}_ n$ finite locally free. Here are some special cases of Proposition <a href="/tag/0EL5" data-tag="0EL5">52.28.5</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $X$ is a curve or a surface, we don't learn anything. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $X$ is a Cohen-Macaulay threefold, then $(\mathcal{E}_ n)$ is the completion of a coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module $\mathcal{E}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
More generally, if $\dim (X) \geq 3$ and $X$ is $(S_3)$, then $(\mathcal{E}_ n)$ is the completion of a coherent $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module $\mathcal{E}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Of course, if $\mathcal{E}$ exists, then $\mathcal{E}$ is finite locally free in an open neighbourhood of $Y$. </p>
</article>